\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The indistinguishable pair behind Theorem\nonbreakingspace \ref  {thm:separation}. The two inputs present the \emph  {same} artifact $e$ but opposite ground truths, differing only in context $c$ (task identity, policy version, provenance) that an artifact-only judge cannot observe. Any such $J$ must return one output on both, so it authorizes an unsafe transition on $c'$ or wrongly declines on $c$---a gap no classification accuracy closes. The binding and ex-ante predicates break the pair by making $c$ observable to the guard.\relax }}{4}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:indist}{{1}{4}{The indistinguishable pair behind Theorem~\ref {thm:separation}. The two inputs present the \emph {same} artifact $e$ but opposite ground truths, differing only in context $c$ (task identity, policy version, provenance) that an artifact-only judge cannot observe. Any such $J$ must return one output on both, so it authorizes an unsafe transition on $c'$ or wrongly declines on $c$---a gap no classification accuracy closes. The binding and ex-ante predicates break the pair by making $c$ observable to the guard.\relax }{figure.caption.9}{}}
